            "source": "real-world durability proof"
          }
        },
        "b5": {
          "type": "feature",
          "settings": {
            "eyebrow": "The science",
            "heading": "Stronger than steel, gram for gram",
            "body": "Bamboo has an exceptional strength-to-weight ratio — pound for pound it rivals metal, at a fraction of the weight. It's a natural composite: parallel cellulose fibres in a lignin matrix running the full length of the culm — the same structural principle as carbon fibre, evolved over millions of years. | We tested this with Swansea University: flexural and tensile tests on our actual bamboo tubes confirmed high stiffness and strength for the weight. And in our frames, every joint is reinforced with carbon-fibre.",
            "image_fallback": "bbc-rd-detail.jpg",
            "image_alt": "Cut bamboo tubes showing the hollow cross-section and dense outer fibre wall",
            "reverse": false,
            "dark": false,
            "cta_label": "Read the Swansea University study",
            "cta_url": "\/blogs\/news\/bamboo-testing-with-swansea-university",
            "quote": "Woody turned out to be the most reliable bike I've ever owned. I had hardly any mechanical problems on the whole journey and it was a smooth and shock-absorbing ride.",
            "attribution": "Kate Rawles",
            "context": "8,288 miles, Colombia to Patagonia, on a BBC-built bamboo frame"
          }
        },
        "b6": {
          "type": "quote",
          "settings": {
            "quote": "Woody turned out to be the most reliable bike I've ever owned. I had hardly any mechanical problems on the whole journey and it was a smooth and shock-absorbing ride.",
            "attribution": "Kate Rawles",
            "context": "8,288 miles, Colombia to Patagonia, on a BBC-built bamboo frame"
          }
        },
        "b7": {
          "type": "feature",
          "settings": {
            "eyebrow": "Ride quality",
            "heading": "A ride you can actually feel",
            "body": "Bamboo is a natural composite — and natural fibres damp vibration better than bare metal, a property documented in materials testing. Paired with the right geometry and tyres, that means a ride riders describe again and again as smooth: less road buzz, less fatigue on long days. | Not a gimmick — a material doing what it is good at. You arrive fresher, with less strain on your hands, wrists and back.",
            "image_fallback": "bbc-rd-gravel-sunset.jpg",
            "image_alt": "A bamboo bike beside a misty mountain valley at dawn",
            "reverse": true,
            "dark": false,
            "cta_label": "",
            "quote": "Having had it now for about 7 years and ridden it hard — the ride is much smoother than my Cooper. I've ridden over about half of Crete with it.",
            "attribution": "Jamie",
            "context": "London workshop builder, 7-year review"
          }
        },
        "b8": {
          "type": "quote",
          "settings": {
            "quote": "Having had it now for about 7 years and ridden it hard — the ride is much smoother than my Cooper. I've ridden over about half of Crete with it.",
            "attribution": "Jamie",
            "context": "London workshop builder, 7-year review"
          }
        },
        "b9": {
          "type": "feature",
          "settings": {
            "eyebrow": "Accessibility",
            "heading": "Why it matters",
            "body": "To make an aluminium frame you need bauxite mines, smelters running near 1,000°C and factories of CNC machines. Carbon fibre needs petroleum-derived precursors, autoclaves and clean rooms. | To make a bamboo bicycle frame you need bamboo, a jig, hand tools and two days. That's it. | We teach beginners — most with no prior experience at all — to build their own frame in a weekend. Through our Build to Bond programme, frame-building reconnects fathers with their children inside the criminal justice system.",
            "image_fallback": "bbc-rd-frame-build.jpg",
            "image_alt": "A bamboo frame on the build jig with hand tools — no factory needed",
            "reverse": false,
            "dark": true,
            "cta_label": "",
            "quote": "I knew nothing about bike maintenance. I thought that if I could be involved in the whole process of putting it together, I'd have a better idea of how to tackle any inevitable breakages. It reminded me how satisfying it is to build something from scratch.",
            "attribution": "Soraya",
            "context": "4-day London workshop, road bamboo bike build"
          }
        },
        "b10": {
          "type": "feature",
          "settings": {
            "eyebrow": "Sustainability",
            "heading": "Grown, not mined",
            "body": "Bamboo reaches structural maturity in 3–5 years where trees take 20–50; it regrows from its own root system with no replanting, and needs no pesticides or irrigation. | But the real sustainability is in your hands: because you build the frame yourself, you learn to repair it — and a bike you can mend is a bike you keep. | Most mass-made frames are thrown away when they fail. A bamboo frame you built and understand? Still being ridden a decade later.",
            "image_fallback": "bbc-rd-bamboo-tubes.jpg",
            "image_alt": "Raw bamboo culms in the workshop — frame-grade in three to five years",
            "reverse": true,
            "dark": false,
            "cta_label": "See how every kit funds learning",
            "cta_url": "\/pages\/impact"
          }
        },
        "b12": {
          "type": "quote",
          "settings": {
            "quote": "I knew nothing about bike maintenance. I thought that if I could be involved in the whole process of putting it together, I'd have a better idea of how to tackle any inevitable breakages. It reminded me how satisfying it is to build something from scratch.",
            "attribution": "Soraya",
            "context": "4-day London workshop, road bamboo bike build"
          }
        },
